      "reasoning": "Design B delivers the brief's condensed futurist numerals, a dramatic glowing lime curve with labeled points and a tightly gridded metric column, while Design A's generic bold sans, off-brand red/cyan chart dots and empty lower-right area weaken both palette compliance and hierarchy."
    },
    {
      "slide": 2,
      "winner": "Design B",
      "reasoning": "Design B is a genuinely illustrated hand-drawn route with clay dominance, marigold accents and a taped note, whereas Design A is a flat centered timeline on blush with large dead zones and a stray tilted buddy box."
    },
    {
      "slide": 3,
      "winner": "Design B",
      "reasoning": "Design B builds a precise enterprise before/after with a tangled vermilion graph versus an ordered azure identity graph, clean rules and aligned metric rows, while Design A's clip-art arrow, overlapping connector lines and floating dots read unresolved."
    },
    {
      "slide": 4,
      "winner": "Design B",
      "reasoning": "Design B's orbital allocation ring with gold segments, centered $240M and restrained serif hierarchy matches the institutional brief exactly, while Design A defaults to a standard donut plus a legend that duplicates the percentages."
    },
    {
      "slide": 5,
      "winner": "Design B",
      "reasoning": "Design B is a true keynote reveal—brushed-silver ground, oversized ARC ONE wordmark, exploded driver stack with signal-red callouts—while Design A relies on a dim generic headphone photo with a hard split edge and small spec list."
    },
    {
      "slide": 6,
      "winner": "Design B",
      "reasoning": "Design B is a coherent monospaced navy scorecard with one revenue-vs-plan curve, variance markers and an emphasized activation gap bar, whereas Design A's descending line misleadingly plots unrelated regions and its 'ACTIVATION / TARGET 75%' labels collide."
    },
    {
      "slide": 7,
      "winner": "Design B",
      "reasoning": "Design B delivers the requested custom transit-map diagram with interchange and north-star measures in wayfinding type, while Design A reduces the pillars to four unequal bar rules and an awkward oversized black 'North Star' circle."
    },
    {
      "slide": 8,
      "winner": "Design B",
      "reasoning": "Design B's stepped chevron swimlanes with citron gate diamonds, week ruler, owners and team dot-count read as a dynamic roadmap, while Design A's four flat blocks with dashed gate lines and unlabeled gate descriptions are closer to the boxy layout the brief warned against."
    },
    {
      "slide": 9,
      "winner": "Design B",
      "reasoning": "Design B renders a bold circular 4C loop with arrowed flow plus a marker-annotated worked example, while Design A's overlapping bubble cluster has cramped text inside circles, disconnected diagonal ticks and a large empty lower half."
    },
    {
      "slide": 10,
      "winner": "Design B",
      "reasoning": "Design B pairs an expressive 100-square 68% waffle with an actual fragmented three-app journey diagram and editorial serif headline, whereas Design A's plain donut and three labeled circles are generic and leave the cobalt quote bar floating."
    },
    {
      "slide": 11,
      "winner": "Design B",
      "reasoning": "Design B captures the auditorium keynote moment with oversized dissolving display type, a real visible/hidden UI diagram and a silhouetted crowd, while Design A stacks a banner headline over dim stock audience photography with awkward boxes and centered all-caps text."
    },
    {
      "slide": 12,
      "winner": "Design B",
      "reasoning": "Design B's orange/powder-blue split with oversized editorial numerals, aligned metric rows and a properly attributed quote is confident and structurally tight, while Design A leaves large orange voids, inconsistent number alignment and a quote box with an oddly stranded label."
    },
    {
      "slide": 13,
      "winner": "Design B",
      "reasoning": "Design B makes an illustrated corridor map the informational centerpiece with scale bar, legend and a field-ledger card in the specified sage/ochre/river-blue palette, while Design A's stock aerial photo with disconnected orange dashes reads as arbitrary graphic overlay rather than a mapped corridor."
    },
    {
      "slide": 14,
      "winner": "Design B",
      "reasoning": "Design B's enormous annotated 'a' with numbered leader lines, yellow counter fill and a same-baseline three-face comparison is genuinely instructional, while Design A's small letterform, floating rule-to-label lines and black spec panel are far less legible as teaching."
    },
    {
      "slide": 15,
      "winner": "Design B",
      "reasoning": "Design B's collage citrus macro, aqua sector bar and dotted leader list form an energetic editorial composition, whereas Design A's photo panel has a visible tonal seam, a transparent overlay box clipping the image edge and near-invisible contact text."
    }
  ]
